---
/*
  A4 - The KleidiAI quantization gate.
  Arm's KleidiAI registers matmul kernels for exactly two GGML types, Q4_0 and
  Q8_0 (ggml/src/ggml-cpu/kleidiai/kleidiai.cpp). Every other quantization
  misses them - silently, until llama.cpp PR #25701 (merged 2026-07-21) added
  the one-time warning. The fallback is not "generic scalar code": K-quants
  still reach ggml's Arm repack kernels (JOURNEY.md section 3); the cost shows
  up where the compute-bound GEMM lives - prompt processing and TTFT.
*/
import Fig from "../Fig.astro";
import CaveatCard from "../CaveatCard.astro";

const quants = [
  { q: "Q4_0", fast: true },
  { q: "Q8_0", fast: true },
  { q: "Q3_K_L", fast: false },
  { q: "Q4_K_M", fast: false },
  { q: "Q5_K_M", fast: false },
  { q: "Q6_K", fast: false },
  { q: "F16", fast: false },
];
---

<Fig
  id="a4"
  title="A4 - The KleidiAI quantization gate"
  shows="A GGUF's header names its quantization; that one field decides whether Arm's KleidiAI kernels run at all. Q4_0 and Q8_0 route to them - everything else drops to ggml's other CPU paths, and until PR #25701 nothing told you."
  data="measured"
  dataNote="Kernel gate verified in ggml/src/ggml-cpu/kleidiai/kleidiai.cpp. Cost table: CMF Phone 1, same 512-token prompt, same 4-thread unpinned config, only the quantization differs - docs/KLEIDIAI-QUANTS.md."
  controls={false}
>
  <div id="a4-root">
    <div role="group" aria-label="Pick a quantization" class="flex flex-wrap gap-1.5">
      {
        quants.map((x, i) => (
          <button
            type="button"
            class="a4-q chip cursor-pointer"
            data-fast={x.fast ? "1" : "0"}
            data-on={i === 0 || undefined}
            aria-pressed={i === 0 ? "true" : "false"}
          >
            {x.q}
          </button>
        ))
      }
    </div>

    <div class="mt-5 grid grid-cols-[auto_auto_1fr] items-center gap-x-3 md:gap-x-5 text-[0.75rem]">
      <div class="a4-node card px-3 py-2">
        <span class="microlabel !text-[0.5625rem]">gguf</span>
        <p id="a4-file" class="font-medium">model-Q4_0.gguf</p>
      </div>
      <div class="a4-node card px-3 py-2">
        <span class="microlabel !text-[0.5625rem]">header read</span>
        <p class="font-medium">general.file_type</p>
      </div>
      <div class="grid gap-3">
        <div
          id="a4-path-fast"
          class="a4-path rounded-[8px] border px-3 py-2 transition-colors duration-300"
          data-active
        >
          <p class="font-bold">KleidiAI kernels <span class="chip !text-[0.5625rem] !px-1.5 ml-1" data-on>Q4_0 &middot; Q8_0 only</span></p>
          <p class="text-dim text-[0.6875rem] mt-0.5">
            Arm-written matmul micro-kernels for the compute-bound prompt GEMM.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div
          id="a4-path-slow"
          class="a4-path rounded-[8px] border px-3 py-2 transition-colors duration-300"
        >
          <p class="font-bold">ggml CPU path - no KleidiAI</p>
          <p class="text-dim text-[0.6875rem] mt-0.5">
            Not bare scalar code: K-quants still reach ggml's Arm dotprod repack kernels. What
            they miss is KleidiAI - and nothing said so until the upstream warning landed.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <p id="a4-verdict" class="mt-4 text-[0.8125rem]">
      <span class="font-bold">Q4_0 reaches Arm's fast path.</span>
      <span class="text-dim"> The app's model card reports this state as "KLEIDIAI ACTIVE".</span>
    </p>

    <div class="scroll-x mt-4">
      <table class="data-table min-w-[520px]">
        <caption class="sr-only">
          Measured cost of missing the fast path: Q3_K_L vs Q4_0, same phone and config
        </caption>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th scope="col"><span class="sr-only">Metric</span></th>
            <th scope="col" class="num">Q3_K_L (733 MB, no fast path)</th>
            <th scope="col" class="num">Q4_0 (773 MB, fast path)</th>
            <th scope="col" class="num">change</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td>Prompt throughput</td>
            <td class="num">42.7 tok/s</td>
            <td class="num font-bold">121 tok/s</td>
            <td class="num t-ok">+183%</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Derived TTFT (512-token prompt)</td>
            <td class="num">12,050 ms</td>
            <td class="num font-bold">4,299 ms</td>
            <td class="num t-ok">-64%</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Decode throughput</td>
            <td class="num">16.9 tok/s</td>
            <td class="num">14.7 tok/s</td>
            <td class="num t-danger">-13%</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
  </div>

  <p slot="caption">
    The fallback used to be silent - no log line, nothing in logcat. This project contributed the
    one-time warning upstream:
    <a href="https://github.com/ggml-org/llama.cpp/pull/25701">llama.cpp PR #25701</a>, merged
    2026-07-21 as commit <code>fb0e6b6</code>.
  </p>
</Fig>

<div class="mt-4">
  <CaveatCard title="What this measures, and what it does not attribute">
    <p>
      The table isolates the <strong>quantization</strong>, not KleidiAI specifically: moving to
      Q4_0 switches on both KleidiAI's kernels and ggml's Arm repack path at once, and the split
      between them has not been measured on this phone. Independent measurements suggest the
      KleidiAI flag adds little at Q4_0; its clear win is at Q8_0 (docs/KLEIDIAI-QUANTS.md).
    </p>
    <p>
      Decode does not improve because it is memory-bandwidth-bound and tracks bytes per weight,
      not kernel quality - Q4_0 is about 6% more bytes than Q3_K_L and lands slightly slower.
      Q4_0 is also a quality tradeoff, which is why ENTITY recommends rather than switches: the
      model card reports which case you are in.
    </p>
  </CaveatCard>
</div>

<style>
  .a4-path {
    border-color: var(--outline);
    border-style: dashed;
  }
  .a4-path > p:first-child {
    color: var(--dim);
  }
  .a4-path[data-active] {
    border-style: solid;
    border-color: var(--fill);
    background: var(--surface);
  }
  .a4-path[data-active] > p:first-child {
    color: var(--fg);
  }
</style>

<script>
  const root = document.getElementById("a4-root");
  if (root) {
    const btns = [...root.querySelectorAll<HTMLButtonElement>(".a4-q")];
    const fast = document.getElementById("a4-path-fast")!;
    const slow = document.getElementById("a4-path-slow")!;
    const file = document.getElementById("a4-file")!;
    const verdict = document.getElementById("a4-verdict")!;
    btns.forEach((b) =>
      b.addEventListener("click", () => {
        btns.forEach((x) => {
          const on = x === b;
          x.toggleAttribute("data-on", on);
          x.setAttribute("aria-pressed", String(on));
        });
        const q = b.textContent!.trim();
        const isFast = b.dataset.fast === "1";
        file.textContent = `model-${q}.gguf`;
        fast.toggleAttribute("data-active", isFast);
        slow.toggleAttribute("data-active", !isFast);
        verdict.innerHTML = isFast
          ? `<span class="font-bold">${q} reaches Arm's fast path.</span> <span class="text-dim">The app's model card reports this state as "KLEIDIAI ACTIVE".</span>`
          : `<span class="font-bold">${q} misses KleidiAI</span> <span class="text-dim">- it runs on ggml's Arm repack kernels instead, and the model card says so before you pay the TTFT.</span>`;
      })
    );
  }
</script>
